<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_mag-challenge" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Challenge - Magnetism</title>
	
	<!--
	If you want an A*R*C*S (Analyze-Represent-Calculate-Sensemake) activity, use the <project> tag.
	If you want an Explain activity, use the <investigation> tag.
		Use this text for qualitative comparisons: <em>greater than</em>, <em>less than</em>, or <em>equal to</em>
	If you want a Calculate activity, use the <exploration> tag.
	
	<m># \mathrm{~}</m>
	<m> \mathrm{~}</m>
	-->

	<investigation>
		<title>Zero Magnetic Field</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<figure xml:id="fig_mag-wires1">
				<caption>Two current carrying wires.</caption>
				<image source="Mag-Wires1.png" width = "50%" ></image>
			</figure>
			<p>
				The two wires shown in the figure above carry identical currents <m>I_1</m>, one into the page and one out of the page.  Each wire is positioned a distance <m>d</m> from the origin at an angle of <m>45^o</m> down from the positive <m>y</m>-axis.
			</p>
			<p>
				You want to position a third wire, carrying current <m>I_1</m> out of the page, in such a way that the net magnetic field at the origin is equal to zero.  Give a complete explanation that determines the location of this third wire.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</investigation>


	<project>
		<title>Current Carrying Half-Cylinder</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<figure xml:id="fig-cylinder-current">
				<caption>Half-cylinder carrying current.</caption>
				<image source="mag/half-cylinder-current.png" width = "40%" ></image>
			</figure>
			<p>
				A very long half-cylinder of radius <m>R</m> (see picture above) carries a total current <m>I</m> uniformly distributed across the circumference.  Calculate the magnetic field along the central axis of the cylinder.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</project>

	<project>
		<title>Solenoid</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<p>
				Consider the magnetic field created by an infinitely long solenoid with radius <m>R</m> and <m>n</m> coils per unit length carrying a steady clockwise current <m>I</m>. Starting from the assumption that the magnetic field everywhere in space must point in either the positive or negative <m>z</m>-direction (parallel to the axis of the solenoid), use Ampere's Law to determine the magnitude and direction of this magnetic field.
			</p>
		</statement>
		<hint>
			<p>
				You will need to choose Amperian Loops thoughtfully, and you may need more than one!
			</p>
		</hint>
	</project>

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Metacognitive Reflection</title>
		<activity>
		<title>Magnetism vs. Electricity</title>
		
		
					<statement><p></p>
			<p>
				Find two activities related to <alert>electric fields</alert> that are similar to the activities you completed this week for <alert>magnetic fields</alert>. Discuss the main similarities and differences between the two fields.
			</p>
		</statement>
		</activity>

	</subsubsection>

<!--NO AI-->
</section>